    "# Assignment 3A — Explore and Plan\n",
    "## Alzheimer's disease: a patient-level exploratory analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook answers all Part A deliverables: planned examinations, data audit, a variable dictionary, selected exploratory analyses, three research questions with plans and preliminary answers, and a data-quality note. The questions are distinct and use only available variables. Results describe this dataset; they do not establish causation, future disease risk, or clinical usefulness.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Run in Colab:** select **Runtime → Run all**, then upload `alzheimers_disease_data.csv` when prompted. A local CSV beside the notebook is also supported. No GPU is needed.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Sources and scope.** The supplied assignment paper defines the required analyses; the supplied data dictionary defines coding and stated ranges. The CSV supplies all results. The [linked Google Sheet](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1zqJ3PM_RNpsgqp_PvD9jfph84sC0nhf-Gh2tHJ51nTs/edit#gid=0), inspected on 24 November 2026, is a class submission register with names and notebook links, not another set of analytical questions. Three linked notebooks were reviewed on 24 November 2026 to identify overlap and assess their methods; the fourth required access. They were not edited. The comparison below paraphrases their approaches; all analysis code and results in this notebook are independently implemented from the supplied CSV. The assignment permits AI implementation and debugging; the submitting student remains responsible for reviewing the analysis and decisions.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Revision:** the original three questions are replaced below. The new questions are distinct from the stated research questions in the three accessible submissions; scientific novelty across the literature has not been established.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "revised-001",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Reference review: what the linked notebooks actually do\n",
    "\n",
    "The [class register](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1zqJ3PM_RNpsgqp_PvD9jfph84sC0nhf-Gh2tHJ51nTs/edit#gid=0) contains four notebook links. Review distinguishes saved code/output from proposed future analysis; none of the reference notebooks was executed or modified.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Reference | Their question | How they investigate it; what is still a plan |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| [A: alzheimers_eda](https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1UMIxcDKgWj3ET795hDis__hhTq5EfYMg) | Lifestyle versus a five-item cognitive/functional flag count | Computes a count using chosen score cutoffs, plots lifestyle means with error bars, and calculates rank correlations. Ordinal regression and alternative-score robustness are proposed, not demonstrated in the inspected code. |\n",
    "| A | Combinations of two or three lifestyle indicators versus diagnosis | Code screens 70 AND/OR combinations, compares binary correlations with their components, and counts Bonferroni-significant results. Continuous interaction models and held-out confirmation are plans, not completed analyses shown in the code. |\n",
    "| A | Family-history modification of other factor–diagnosis associations | Code compares percentage-point differences across family-history strata and plots them. Adjusted regression and alternative-cutoff sensitivity remain proposed. |\n",
    "| [B: DSPY_task3a_alzheimers](https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1WmzgN4QzO0RhnmVtSWO7hNCMDddpP4hU) | Which cognitive/functional score best separates diagnosis groups? | EDA shows group summaries and boxplots. ROC AUC, bootstrap intervals and score ranking are proposed. |\n",
    "| B | Lifestyle/cardiometabolic factors versus diagnosis or MMSE | EDA includes unadjusted comparisons. Multivariable models, false-discovery correction and exclusion sensitivity are proposed. |\n",
    "| B | Age and education versus cognition | EDA shows age-group diagnosis proportions and demographic summaries. Adjusted MMSE models and age-by-education interaction are proposed. |\n",
    "| [C: Assignment_3A_EDA](https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1Z2Hys4SOdQfHqm0O0b8372Q3usVUPDDF) | Cognitive/functional scores versus diagnosis | Group plots and effect sizes are present; a shallow-tree cross-validation check is also run (saved accuracy 0.927 for five assessment variables versus 0.646 majority baseline). Proposed ROC and logistic-model comparisons are not shown as completed. |\n",
    "| C | Memory complaints and behavioral problems versus diagnosis | Four-group counts/rates and a plot are present (approximately 23%, 54%, 61%, 84% diagnosed). Adjusted logistic interaction analysis remains a plan. |\n",
    "| C | Expected risk factors versus diagnosis | Descriptive comparisons, age-group plots and a correlation matrix are present; adjusted logistic analyses remain a plan. |\n",
    "| [D: fourth linked file](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1gWlj51vjyS8FaolemSXQvIqa6aY4gJUY/view) | Not assessable | The connector returned not found, and the browser displayed “You need access.” No content or originality comparison is claimed for this submission. |\n",
    "\n",
    "**Audit and EDA lessons.** A checks duplicates without IDs, category coding, ranges, precision, blood-pressure and lipid consistency, and outliers; it retains flagged rows. B checks missingness, duplicates, types, score precision, blood-pressure ordering and a cholesterol approximation, then uses distributions and grouped summaries. C adds similar audits, numerical distributions, symptom combinations and a small classification exercise. These are useful checks, but range validity, normality/outlier rules and biological consistency answer different questions.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Corrections and limits worth retaining:** a large uniformity-test p-value does not prove that data were generated uniformly; a continuous-uniform test is not the right reference distribution for integer ages. Missing clinical correlations do not prove synthetic provenance. A single high pressure measurement is not enough to declare the history flag erroneous, because timing and treatment are unknown. Low cognitive scores without Alzheimer's diagnosis are not automatically mislabeled. B's text says memory complaints are more common among diagnosed patients, but its displayed 64% versus 28% table actually measures diagnosis frequency conditional on complaint status—the denominator matters. A repeats its lifestyle-combination code cell; this revision avoids that duplication. References B and C largely present *plans* for their three questions, so a proposed model should not be described as an answered analysis.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why change our questions?** The earlier diagnosis–MMSE and age/education questions overlapped with B/C, and activity–ADL overlapped broadly with A's lifestyle/assessment theme. The replacement questions focus on measurement agreement and robustness. The weak ADL–FunctionalAssessment correlation was already noted in the examples' audits; the new contribution is to quantify patient-level rank disagreement and threshold sensitivity, not claim to discover that weak correlation first. No wording or code is copied from those submissions.\n"
   ]
  },
