    "## 1. Plan before implementation\n",
    "\n",
    "All **five examination bullets** in assignment section 1 are covered below.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Examination | Planned approach | Location |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Dimensions, names, types, representative rows | Shape, schema, beginning/middle/end rows | 2 |\n",
    "| Unit of observation and variable roles | Check identifier uniqueness; separate measurements, groups, and possible outcomes | 2–3 |\n",
    "| Semantic variable types | Classify all 35 fields independently of storage dtype | 3 |\n",
    "| Unique values, category counts, ranges and summaries | Counts/proportions for coded categories; descriptive statistics for measurements | 4–5 |\n",
    "| Missingness, duplicates, constants, suspicious values, precision and coding | Explicit checks against dictionary and cross-field flags | 4 |\n",
    "\n",
    "**Additional checks:** duplicates excluding the identifier; identifier sequence versus stated bounds; finite numeric values; blank strings; systolic/diastolic ordering; LDL versus total cholesterol; fractional assessment scores; outcome proportion by identifier order; source fingerprint; and separate inspection of the optional HDF5 file.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Analysis decisions made before interpretation:** preserve original values; flag suspect records rather than silently correcting or deleting them; exclude identifiers and the constant doctor placeholder from substantive relationships; treat ethnicity and gender as nominal, education as ordinal, and binary indicators as categorical. Use a small number of plots with meaningful scales. Use Spearman correlation only for selected measurements. For functional disagreement, prespecify absolute within-sample percentile-rank gaps of 25 points (main) and 50 points (sensitivity); these are descriptive cutoffs, not clinical thresholds. For quality sensitivity, use the union of systolic ≤ diastolic and LDL + HDL > total cholesterol flags. Retain everyone in the primary analyses. Do not fit a predictive model for this exploratory assignment.\n"
   ]
