   "source": [
    "## 6. Three distinct research questions and their answers\n",
    "\n",
    "These questions were selected after reviewing the examples, so the analysis is explicitly exploratory. Their definitions and decision rules appear before the corresponding calculations. We avoid screening hundreds of associations for attractive p-values. Bootstrap intervals describe resampling variability under independent-record sampling; they do not resolve undocumented provenance or establish representativeness. All primary results use the complete dataset.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Question 1 — Do MemoryComplaints and Forgetfulness identify the same records, and how do their four combinations differ in MMSE?\n",
    "\n",
    "**Rationale:** similarly named fields may not measure the same construct. Agreement is a different question from whether either predicts diagnosis, which the reference notebooks emphasize.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Variables:** MemoryComplaints and Forgetfulness (binary indicators); MMSE (numerical comparison outcome). Diagnosis is not used to define groups.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Plan:** create the full 2×2 table with counts and row percentages. Report overall agreement, Cohen's kappa (agreement beyond that expected from marginal proportions), positive agreement and negative agreement. Compare MMSE count, mean, median and quartiles for neither indicator, complaints only, forgetfulness only and both. Bootstrap the two summary contrasts: kappa and mean MMSE for both minus neither, with 1,000 fixed-seed resamples.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Visualization:** 2×2 count heatmap and four-group MMSE boxplot.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Limitations:** neither indicator is a gold standard, so this is not sensitivity/specificity. Different recall periods, reporting sources or definitions could produce disagreement. Kappa depends on marginal frequencies. MMSE is not an independent diagnosis, and fractional scores require source clarification. No causal or clinical threshold is used.\n"
   ]
  },
