   "source": [
    "### Question 2 — How often do ADL and FunctionalAssessment place the same patient at substantially different positions within the sample?\n",
    "\n",
    "**Rationale:** the examples note a small correlation between these fields. Correlation alone does not show how many records receive different relative rankings. This question develops that audit observation into an explicit agreement analysis; it is not a claim to be first to notice the correlation.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Variables:** ADL and FunctionalAssessment, both scored 0–10 with lower values indicating more impairment. No outcome is privileged.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Plan:** convert each score to its within-sample percentile rank (average ranks for ties). Compute Spearman correlation, the median absolute rank gap, and the proportion whose absolute rank gap exceeds 25 percentile points. Repeat at a stricter 50-point threshold. Use a 4×4 quartile cross-tabulation. Bootstrap the rank correlation. Do not equate raw scores merely because both scales range from 0 to 10.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Visualization:** percentile-rank scatterplot and absolute rank-gap histogram. The diagonal is equal relative rank, not a clinical equivalence line.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Limitations:** ranks depend on this sample, and cutoffs are analyst choices, not validated impairment categories. Different instruments can measure different aspects of functioning. Poor agreement does not prove either score is wrong. A common 0–10 range does not justify treating the scales as interchangeable or using a raw-score agreement test without more documentation.\n"
   ]
  },
