    "### Question 3 — Do the measurement-agreement conclusions change when records with cross-field quality flags are excluded?\n",
    "\n",
    "**Rationale:** detecting questionable values is only the first audit step. This question tests the practical sensitivity of the two main conclusions to a transparent exclusion rule, rather than assuming that removal improves validity.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Variables:** quality flags from SystolicBP, DiastolicBP, CholesterolLDL, CholesterolHDL and CholesterolTotal; primary endpoints are memory-indicator kappa and the ADL–FunctionalAssessment rank correlation. The >25-point functional rank-gap proportion is a secondary endpoint.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Plan:** report separate and overlapping flags. Compare the full sample with records having neither flag, and also report flagged records separately. Recalculate kappa and rank correlation. Keep full-sample ranks fixed for the threshold-based secondary endpoint so that the exclusion does not redefine the rank-gap criterion. Bootstrap the *change* (unflagged minus full) by resampling whole rows and applying the same exclusion rule within each draw. Use 1,000 draws with a fixed seed. Before calculation, call a change at least 0.10 in kappa/rho, or at least 5 percentage points in the threshold-based proportion, descriptively material. These tolerances are transparent analyst choices, not validated equivalence margins.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Visualization:** full-sample and unflagged point estimates with a table of paired-bootstrap change intervals.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Limitations:** unflagged does not mean clinically verified. Removing a third of the data may introduce selection bias, and conflicts in BP/lipids need not invalidate memory or functioning measurements. The restricted sample is nested in the full sample; independent-sample tests would be inappropriate. Passing a sensitivity check establishes robustness only to this rule, not the truth of the measurements. Threshold-based intervals condition on the original sample's rank definitions.\n"
   ]
  },
